    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let \\( l \\) be the length and \\( w \\) be the width of the backyard. We are given two scenarios:\n\n1. Mateen walks the length 25 times:\n\\[ \\text{Total distance walked} = 25 \\times l = 1000 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\\[ l = \\frac{1000}{25} = 40 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\n2. Mateen walks the perimeter 10 times:\n\\[ \\text{Total distance walked} = 10 \\times (2l + 2w) = 1000 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\\[ 2l + 2w = \\frac{1000}{10} = 100 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\\[ l + w = \\frac{100}{2} = 50 \\text{ meters} \\]\n\nNow, we have two equations:\n\\[ l = 40 \\]\n\\[ l + w = 50 \\]\n\nTo find \\( w \\), substitute \\( l = 40 \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ 40 + w = 50 \\]\n\\[ w = 50 - 40 \\]\n\\[ w = 10 \\]\n\nThe area \\( A \\) of the backyard is given by:\n\\[ A = l \\times w = 40 \\times 10 = 400 \\text{ square meters} \\]\n\nSo, the area of Mateen's backyard is \\( \\boxed{400} \\) square meters.",
